      body: [
        "First of all, let's sharpen the basic words in phase 6, because in the easy questions of the exam, marks will be lost by confusing these words. Zero-shot describes the work, but does not give any solved examples (solved demonstrations). One-shot is exactly one example. Few-shot means giving several pairs of input → output, and finally giving a new input without the output. If there is only the last answer in the examples, it is few-shot. It is not a chain-of-thought, because the middle steps are not shown. Instruction tuning is not a method of writing prompts, it is a process of training the model.",
        "In-context learning (ICL) means changing the behavior of the model by looking at the examples in the prompt. The most important thing: none of the weights will change. Everything happens inside the forward pass. When the context is removed, what was learned is also lost. Therefore, the options \"taking several gradient steps\" (that's fine-tuning), \"bringing examples from a vector database\" (that's retrieval), \"remembering new facts permanently\" are all wrong.",
        "There are three (sometimes four) roles in a Chat API. The system message gives the rules, persona, and constraints that affect the entire conversation. Instruction-tuned models usually give it more priority. The user message is the end user's problem. Assistant messages contain answers given by the model before. Also, you can \"prefill\" an assistant message by writing it yourself, make fake turns and give them as demonstrations. Tool results come from the tool role. A prompt template is a regular text with placeholders like \"Summarize: {text}\", which can be used repeatedly, versioned and tested. It is not something trained like an adapter.",
        "When using an open instruction-tuned model, if you add a string like \"User: \" + question + \"\\nAssistant:\" the output will be confused. Reason: The model is trained with a chat template that has its own special tokens and role markers. The same format is created by tokenizer.apply_chat_template(). add_generation_prompt=When set to True, the tokens to start the assistant turn will also be added at the end. If you buy a tokenizer from another model family, the token IDs will be wrong.",
        "There are also questions about generation settings. Temperature close to 0 (or greedy decoding) is good for tasks that have only one correct answer, such as extracting fields from invoices. Frequency penalty only reduces repetition, not determinism. If the summary is cut in the middle of the sentence, max_tokens (max new tokens) is less. The API shows the finish reason as \"length\". A stop sequence means to stop as soon as that string is generated (for example, stop when \"\\nUser:\" comes, or the model writes the user's next turn as well). To completely ban a word, logit bias or constrained decoding is required. Finally, remember the order: prompt engineering (cheapest, fastest) first. If you want new knowledge and citations like policy documents that change from week to week, RAG. Fine-tuning if you want the same style/format in millions of calls with a short prompt. Pre-training is never the first step.",
      ],
      analogy: "Imagine a new cashier. If the manager just says \"fix the bill\", it's a zero-shot. If you say \"here is a previous bill, make it like that\" it is one-shot. Few-shot if three or four Bills are shown. His brain (weights) did not change. Just looking at it today (ICL). I don't remember those bills when I go home. The rulebook (system prompt) of the shop is always available at the counter.",
      example: "Messages you send to a Chat API: [system: \"You are a Dialog support agent. Answer briefly.\"], [user: \"Why is my bill Rs. 2,450?\"], [assistant: \"In your package...\"], [user: \"Can it be reduced?\"]. Model has no such thing as \"memory\". We know that because we resend the previous assistant answer. You can put temperature=0, max_tokens=300, stop=[\"\\nUser:\"] for an extraction.",
